WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.558
Whole-farm simulation models predict milk, feed intake, methane and manure.

2
00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:08.853
And almost all of it hangs on one small equation:

3
00:00:08.903 --> 00:00:10.171
the lactation curve.

4
00:00:10.571 --> 00:00:13.346
The model here, RuFaS, uses Wood's curve.

5
00:00:13.396 --> 00:00:14.556
Three parameters:

6
00:00:14.606 --> 00:00:18.927
a sets the scale, b the rise to peak, and c the decline after it.

7
00:00:20.577 --> 00:00:32.089
By default, the parameters come from national averages, built from more than eight million Holstein lactations, then adjusted for region, calving season, milking frequency and parity.

8
00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:37.470
But two farms with identical metadata can produce very different amounts of milk.

9
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.826
Genetics, diets, reproduction and culling are invisible to that method.

10
00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:48.846
This paper adds a calibration step that needs only three numbers a farm already knows:

11
00:00:48.896 --> 00:00:53.581
annual herd milk production, the number of milking cows, and the parity mix.

12
00:00:54.281 --> 00:00:59.230
First, turn annual herd milk into a three-hundred-and-five-day yield per cow.

13
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.115
Then split it by parity, using fixed ratios:

14
00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:08.204
second parity makes eighteen percent more than first, third and later twenty-five percent more.

15
00:01:10.154 --> 00:01:13.676
Second, for each parity, keep b and c, and search over a.

16
00:01:13.726 --> 00:01:21.560
Step it by one hundredth, integrate the curve over three hundred and five days, and keep the value that best matches the target.

17
00:01:23.546 --> 00:01:28.463
The test used ten commercial Holstein farms in New York, Texas and Wisconsin.

18
00:01:28.513 --> 00:01:36.297
With the default method, simulated milk landed between eighty and a hundred and twenty percent of observed for most farms.

19
00:01:36.347 --> 00:01:41.074
Two farms came out at one hundred sixty-seven, and two hundred six percent.

20
00:01:41.474 --> 00:01:45.859
After calibration, every farm falls within four percent of observed.

21
00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:51.451
Root mean square percentage error drops from forty point six percent, to two point two.

22
00:01:53.751 --> 00:01:55.448
And it matters downstream.

23
00:01:55.498 --> 00:02:05.221
Milk drives intake, enteric methane and manure, so the estimated greenhouse gas intensity shifts by sixteen percent on average between the two methods.

24
00:02:05.621 --> 00:02:06.391
The caveats.

25
00:02:06.441 --> 00:02:10.176
Only milk production could be checked against farm records.

26
00:02:10.226 --> 00:02:12.573
The method is built on Holstein data.

27
00:02:12.623 --> 00:02:15.790
And it calibrates the herd, not the individual cow.

28
00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.036
The takeaway:

29
00:02:19.086 --> 00:02:25.180
one number the farm already has, annual milk, makes the whole simulation far more farm-specific.
